00:07:46.527 --> 00:07:49.473
- My question's just
circling around our culture

00:07:49.473 --> 00:07:51.037
and our growth.

00:07:51.037 --> 00:07:53.108
Year after year, and I've
been here for a year,

00:07:53.108 --> 00:07:56.401
and just seen our culture
grow twice in its size.

00:07:56.401 --> 00:08:00.078
Any impacts or learnings
that you've taken away

00:08:00.078 --> 00:08:02.496
that have just shaped how
we're looking forward,

00:08:02.496 --> 00:08:04.162
and how we're staffing our business,

00:08:04.162 --> 00:08:06.562
how we're maintaining our culture?

00:08:06.562 --> 00:08:08.062
- I think the biggest thing I learned

00:08:08.062 --> 00:08:10.358
over the last 18 months as
we've had a lot of growth

00:08:10.358 --> 00:08:13.467
is that, if the intent is right,

00:08:13.467 --> 00:08:15.011
and look, you being the head of HR,

00:08:15.011 --> 00:08:18.485
you have such a view that
so many other people don't,

00:08:18.485 --> 00:08:22.203
which is, you get to see in the
closed door meetings with me

00:08:22.203 --> 00:08:26.443
the consistent North Star.

00:08:26.443 --> 00:08:28.162
I don't know, you've had HR jobs before.

00:08:28.162 --> 00:08:29.767
I feel like a lot more people

00:08:29.767 --> 00:08:31.555
make a lot more business decisions,

00:08:31.555 --> 00:08:33.789
and the money is a big variable

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:36.635
in, let's not give this
person any more time,

00:08:36.635 --> 00:08:39.905
or let's fire this person,
or let's promote this person.

00:08:39.905 --> 00:08:42.405
Money, I think you've sniffed out,

00:08:42.405 --> 00:08:45.412
is so not a variable in those decisions.

00:08:45.412 --> 00:08:47.870
I think what's happened in the last year,

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:49.495
to answer your question directly,

00:08:49.495 --> 00:08:52.787
I think I'm even more confident than ever

00:08:52.787 --> 00:08:56.200
that if you're intent is right,

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:58.331
and you're committed to
that intent at the top,

00:08:59.471 --> 00:09:02.863
that you can scale that,
whether it's 20 people.

00:09:02.863 --> 00:09:04.713
Look, there's a ton of
people watching right now

00:09:04.713 --> 00:09:07.170
that work in offices of 27 people,

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:09.101
and their culture sucks shit.

00:09:09.101 --> 00:09:10.930
The amount of people here
that have worked at places

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:12.413
that had 29 people.

00:09:12.413 --> 00:09:13.835
I just met somebody the other day

00:09:13.835 --> 00:09:15.887
who was like, "I just
came from another agency,

00:09:15.887 --> 00:09:17.187
and our culture was so bad."

00:09:17.187 --> 00:09:18.447
They had 16 people.

00:09:18.447 --> 00:09:20.865
So I don't think the number of employees

00:09:20.865 --> 00:09:23.587
is the variable of a culture.

00:09:23.587 --> 00:09:26.431
I think it's cliche and
accepted that, when you grow,

00:09:26.431 --> 00:09:27.710
you can lose it.

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:29.438
My belief, 'cause I'm in a lot of startups

00:09:29.438 --> 00:09:30.861
that have grown very big,

00:09:30.861 --> 00:09:32.283
is that, what happens is,

00:09:32.283 --> 00:09:35.614
the CEO or the founders take
their eye off of the culture,

00:09:35.614 --> 00:09:37.850
and they start looking for the exit.

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:39.617
All of a sudden, if you're looking to sell

00:09:39.617 --> 00:09:43.091
to Google, or Facebook,
or to another agency,

00:09:43.091 --> 00:09:45.712
you don't care about the
people, you care about the exit.

00:09:45.712 --> 00:09:47.520
And then the culture hurts.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:49.700
- So how do you define culture then?

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:52.309
- I think it's the collective
feeling of everybody.

00:09:52.309 --> 00:09:55.600
To me, culture is a backwards thing of,

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.698
do the people here actually
want to come to work,

00:09:58.698 --> 00:10:01.786
and actually like it,
and it's not a chore,

00:10:01.786 --> 00:10:04.650
or it's not super stressful?

00:10:04.650 --> 00:10:07.453
To me, it's how each individual feels

00:10:07.453 --> 00:10:09.283
that adds up to one collective feeling.

00:10:09.283 --> 00:10:10.461
- Right, perfect.

00:10:10.461 --> 00:10:11.822
- Awesome, thanks.
- [Minnie] Thank you.

00:10:11.822 --> 00:10:13.142
- India, now what?

00:10:13.142 --> 00:10:14.078
Last one?

00:10:14.078 --> 00:10:14.829
- From Steve.

00:10:14.829 --> 00:10:15.926
- Steve.

00:10:15.926 --> 00:10:16.847
- Steve.

00:10:18.283 --> 00:10:19.410
You wanna come in, ask?

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:20.386
- Yeah.

00:10:20.386 --> 00:10:21.138
'Sup, Gary?

00:10:22.258 --> 00:10:24.226
So my question is,

00:10:24.226 --> 00:10:25.771
you're always super busy,

00:10:25.771 --> 00:10:27.639
always looking to take on more.

00:10:27.639 --> 00:10:29.088
How do you do it all?

00:10:33.085 --> 00:10:35.468
- Let's let this guy answer that.

00:10:38.031 --> 00:10:41.221
- Steve, that was a pretty good question,

00:10:41.221 --> 00:10:44.431
but a really simple answer
to the question, which is,

00:10:44.431 --> 00:10:45.954
it just comes down to time.

00:10:45.954 --> 00:10:47.560
At the end of the day, Steve,

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:49.348
because you've worked at
VaynerMedia for four years,

00:10:49.348 --> 00:10:51.541
I know that you work, I don't know,

00:10:51.541 --> 00:10:53.167
seven, eight, nine hours.

00:10:53.167 --> 00:10:54.366
I work 18.

00:10:54.366 --> 00:10:57.595
Just the volume of hours
allows me to do a lot more.

00:10:57.595 --> 00:10:58.937
Steve, think about it this way.

00:10:58.937 --> 00:11:01.788
I'm probably living twice
the life that you are.

00:11:01.788 --> 00:11:02.933
What it comes down to is,

00:11:02.933 --> 00:11:05.819
there's just a lot more
hours of execution,

00:11:05.819 --> 00:11:10.023
and then also recognizing how
few things actually matter.

00:11:10.023 --> 00:11:12.035
One of the things, as you
know as a Vayner employee,

00:11:12.035 --> 00:11:14.210
we had a company-wide meeting once

00:11:14.210 --> 00:11:16.770
talking about, let's cut meetings in half.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:18.475
All those hour meetings can be 30.

00:11:18.475 --> 00:11:20.406
All those 30 meetings can be 15.

00:11:20.406 --> 00:11:22.499
At the end of the day, I
think the biggest thing

00:11:22.499 --> 00:11:23.779
that people don't understand is,

00:11:23.779 --> 00:11:25.689
they waste time on dumb shit.

00:11:25.689 --> 00:11:27.335
So if you have six minute meetings,

00:11:27.335 --> 00:11:30.443
if you have a two minute call
when it's scheduled for 15,

00:11:30.443 --> 00:11:32.597
and when you actually work 18 hours a day,

00:11:32.597 --> 00:11:35.116
you can just fit in a lot more stuff.

00:11:35.116 --> 00:11:37.920
I think it comes down
to, ultimately, intent.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:39.464
How much do you want to accomplish?

00:11:39.464 --> 00:11:41.028
What do you want to get done?

00:11:41.028 --> 00:11:43.202
And for me, that's a lot
of professional stuff.

00:11:43.202 --> 00:11:45.640
In those massive amounts of hours,

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:48.891
if I maximize the width,
18 hours versus nine,

00:11:48.891 --> 00:11:50.963
and if I fit 97 things in,

00:11:50.963 --> 00:11:54.458
because you really only need
seven minutes, not 15 or 30,

00:11:54.458 --> 00:11:56.713
the amount of lollygagging that goes on

00:11:56.713 --> 00:11:58.906
by 99 percent of the market in meetings

00:11:58.906 --> 00:12:01.630
or the things they're supposed
to do is extraordinary.

00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:04.129
So keeping it tight and
creating a lot of volume

00:12:04.129 --> 00:12:06.140
is how I execute.

00:12:06.140 --> 00:12:07.806
Pretty special show, India.

00:12:07.806 --> 00:12:10.082
Episode 151, pretty excited about it.

00:12:10.082 --> 00:12:13.190
The future is very much here, clearly.

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:15.225
I hope you enjoyed it.

00:12:15.225 --> 00:12:16.770
We enjoyed making it.

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:18.375
You keep asking questions.

00:12:18.375 --> 00:12:20.454
I'll keep answering them.

